export anchor TestVerdict:
    description:
        The verdict is the last line in the tool's answer that carries
        PITON-VERDICT followed by a JSON object with a boolean pass, a reason,
        and, when pass is false, an explanation and a fix, even
        wrapped in backticks. So the request echoed back, whose example isn't
        valid JSON, doesn't count, and a verdict the tool revised is replaced
        by the revision. For Claude the answer is the text of its messages
        and its final result; for the other tools it is what they print, on
        stdout first, then stderr. The reason is the verdict's reason, and is
        empty when it gives none.

        A test against both is judged in the specification and in the code
        separately, so its verdict also carries a result for each, spec and
        code, each with its own pass and reason.

        ```text
        \\\
        PITON-VERDICT: {"pass": false, "reason": "The code has no save handler", "spec": {"pass": true, "reason": "The spec says Save saves"}, "code": {"pass": false, "reason": "No handler saves the document"}, "explanation": "...", "fix": "..."}
        \\\
        ```

        With both results, the test passes only when both pass, whatever its
        overall pass says, so a tool can't pass a test whose specification and
        code disagree: true in one and false in the other is a failure. A
        verdict without both results falls back to its overall pass. A test
        against spec or code alone has one result, and any results for each
        side in its verdict are ignored.

        A session that exits with an error, or ends without a verdict, is an
        error rather than a failure: the test was never judged. The error
        says which: the tool's exit code with the last three lines it wrote
        to stderr, or to stdout when stderr is empty, or that it ended
        without a verdict line.
